WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:11.359
The thing to remember about photogrammetry is that not all objects scan well. This is especially true with objects that have reflective or transparent surfaces.

2
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:18.300
Photogrammetry software work by finding feature points that are common between pictures taken at various angles.

3
00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.620
If these common points change between one picture and the next, then the process fails.

4
00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:30.219
This inevitably happens when dealing with reflective and or transparent surfaces.

5
00:00:31.079 --> 00:00:37.579
It also happens when surfaces are very dark, plain colored or otherwise devoid of feature points.

6
00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:42.130
There are workarounds that result with varying degrees of success.

7
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:50.030
One common method is to coat the object with a layer of thin powder, such as chalk spray or dry shampoo.

8
00:00:51.079 --> 00:00:57.079
While these methods may give reasonable results, they can also be messy and a bit hard to clean.

9
00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.460
There are also some commercial products that are known to work well, as they are engineered for that purpose.

10
00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:12.459
They are also easier to clean. In fact, the substance powdery surface disappears by itself a couple of hours after application.

11
00:01:14.579 --> 00:01:20.579
Another method of reducing reflections, glare and even shadows is cross-polarization.

12
00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:29.840
This involves using polarized lenses on both your light and your camera, adding a bit of specialized hardware to your budget.

13
00:01:30.599 --> 00:01:39.599
By using two polarized lenses and rotating them against each other, you significantly reduce artifacts that can make your scan fail.

14
00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:46.219
Ideally, you would use a ring flash for this process, coupled with a polarized lens.

15
00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:54.219
These ring flashes are typically powerful enough to mitigate the effect of direct sunlight, making them useful for external shots.

16
00:01:54.719 --> 00:02:00.540
They would remove all instances of natural shadows, giving you true albedo textures in the process.

17
00:02:02.379 --> 00:02:05.379
You would also need a polarized filter for your camera.

18
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:12.539
Fortunately, polarized DSLR camera filters are quite common in photography and easy to come by.

19
00:02:13.039 --> 00:02:19.919
Ultimately, when you have all your hardware in place and after you've taken pictures, you still need to process those.

20
00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:26.020
There are various solutions out there, some are free, others are commercial.

21
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:34.180
Here we'll concentrate on Epic Games' own photogrammetry solutions and in particular the desktop app known as RealityCapture.

22
00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:42.430
But in the next video, I will discuss photogrammetry on smartphones, so you are at least familiar with that process and options.
